{
  "node_id": "EL.4",
  "title": "Graph logarithmic functions; state domain",
  "mini_lesson": [
    "A research bay's dashboard loses every trace but the largest on a linear scale, and a logarithmic axis brings the whole spread back into one window. What squeezes so wide a spread onto one axis is the logarithm, which hands back an exponent, and exponents creep up while their powers race away. The graph of $y = \\log_{b} x$ is the graph of $y = b^{x}$ reflected across the line $y = x$, so every feature trades places: it takes only positive inputs, hands back every value, and plunges along a vertical line where the exponential flattened along a horizontal one.",
    "As the input to $y = \\log x$ shrinks toward zero the outputs fall with no floor under them, because each further tenth of the input takes one more away from the exponent. No input ever reaches zero, since no exponent takes $10$ to zero, so the curve runs down beside the line $x = 0$ forever without touching it, which makes that line the graph's vertical asymptote. It is the exponential's horizontal asymptote stood upright, since reflecting a flattening across $y = x$ turns it into a plunge.",
    "A logarithm's argument has to be strictly positive, because it stands for an exponent and no power of a positive base ever lands on zero or below. A logarithm's domain (the set of inputs it accepts) is therefore whatever keeps its argument above zero. Shifting the argument shifts that boundary with it, and the vertical asymptote stands at exactly the boundary, so the two ideas are one idea: the asymptote is the wall the domain begins at.",
    "A minus sign in front of the variable turns the domain around: for $y = \\log(h - x)$ the allowed inputs run away to the left of the boundary instead of to the right, while the asymptote still stands at $x = h$. The test itself never changes, which is the point: put the argument above zero, solve for $x$, and whichever direction the solving leaves behind is the direction the domain runs. Set-builder notation writes the two cases as $\\{x \\mid x > h\\}$ and $\\{x \\mid x < h\\}$, each naming every $x$ for which the condition after the bar holds."
  ],
  "slide_subtitles": [
    "The exponential, reflected",
    "What happens near zero?",
    "Which inputs are allowed?",
    "When the side flips"
  ],
  "worked_examples": [
    {
      "prompt": "On the dashboard's logarithmic axis the weakest trace reads $10^{-3}$ and the strongest reads $10^{3}$. How far apart are the intensities behind those two readings?",
      "steps": [
        "The two readings are exponents on base $10$, and they sit six units apart on the axis.",
        "Comparing the intensities themselves divides them: $\\dfrac{10^{3}}{10^{-3}} = 10^{6}$.",
        "So one intensity is a million times the other, and six units of axis have held that whole spread.",
        "Reflecting $y = 10^{x}$ across $y = x$ is what buys the squeeze: the point $(6, 10^{6})$ on the exponential becomes $(10^{6}, 6)$ on the logarithm."
      ]
    },
    {
      "prompt": "Track $y = \\log x$ at $x = 0.1$, then $0.01$, then $0.001$, and name the line the curve runs beside.",
      "steps": [
        "The first input gives $\\log 0.1 = -1$, because $10^{-1} = 0.1$.",
        "The next two give $\\log 0.01 = -2$ and $\\log 0.001 = -3$, so each further tenth of the input costs one more from the output.",
        "Continuing that way, an input of $10^{-20}$ hands back $-20$, and the outputs fall with no floor under them.",
        "The inputs shrink toward $0$ without arriving, so the curve presses against the line $x = 0$, its vertical asymptote."
      ]
    },
    {
      "prompt": "State the domain of $y = \\log(x - 7)$ and give its vertical asymptote.",
      "steps": [
        "The argument is $x - 7$, and a logarithm takes it only while it is above zero: $x - 7 > 0$.",
        "Solving adds $7$ to both sides, which leaves $x > 7$.",
        "So the domain is every $x$ above $7$: an input of $8$ is allowed, while $7$ and $6$ are not.",
        "The boundary the domain begins at is the vertical asymptote, $x = 7$."
      ]
    },
    {
      "prompt": "State the domain of $y = \\log(12 - x)$ in set-builder notation and give its vertical asymptote.",
      "steps": [
        "The argument is $12 - x$, so the condition is $12 - x > 0$.",
        "Adding $x$ to both sides gives $12 > x$, which reads $x < 12$.",
        "The allowed inputs run to the left of the boundary, so the domain is $\\{x \\mid x < 12\\}$.",
        "The argument still hits zero at that boundary, so the vertical asymptote is $x = 12$."
      ]
    }
  ],
